\section{Preparation}{

Names and organizations are removed; organizations are labelled A to G.
Seven people who failed a data-quality screen are excluded: two whose
adjective ratings barely varied (proportion of maximum possible SD below
.25), and five raters who gave the same influence rating to long runs of
consecutive members (28 or more). Their influence ratings are also left
out of everyone's \code{power}. Item properties average eight judges; a
ninth, whose ratings agreed poorly with the others', was dropped.
}

\section{ID linkage in organizations B and D}{

Diagnostics suggest that in two organizations the same ID does not always
refer to the same person across records. \code{people$linkageIssue} is
\code{TRUE} for both.
\itemize{
  \item \strong{D}: at Time 1, ratings of member \emph{i} by \emph{j} are
    unrelated to ratings of \emph{j} by \emph{i} (reciprocity near zero,
    versus .2 to .7 elsewhere), suggesting that survey IDs and roster IDs
    differ. The same ID's Time 1 and Time 2 self-descriptions also match no
    better than random pairs.
  \item \strong{B}: power received barely correlates across waves (about
    .15, versus .5 to .8 elsewhere), even for returning members whose
    self-descriptions confirm they are the same person, suggesting that
    roster IDs changed between waves.
}
Both organizations were included in Wood and Harms (2017), and including
them reproduces the published results closely. Consider reporting results
with and without them.
}

